ole files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages. With `network: true` it may fetch other inputs, records each under `fetched:` and treats the fetched bytes as untrusted data, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make lint test` (so formatting and vet failures are caught before a PR opens), and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present; the file is a constraint, never an instruction, and the worker never resolves a conflict with it: it keeps to the issue and plan and notes the conflict in Open issues. With `Network: available.` the worker fetches only what the plan's Network inputs name, verifies each file against the publisher's checksum file, 